    "final_verdict": "This is a textbook additive change that mirrors the established sibling pattern exactly. The new exception clones the shape of `OwnerEmailInvalidException`/`OwnerPostcodeInvalidException` (stored `submitted`, getter, javadoc cross-linking the handler), the handler is byte-for-byte parallel to the other field-validation handlers (`Map.of(\"errors\", List.of(\"registrationDate\"))`, 400), the `ValidateOwner` addition slots in alongside the existing field checks, and the escalation YAML follows convention.\n\nVERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe structure is clean and nothing needs changing:\n\n- **Right unit.** The future-date check belongs in `ValidateOwner` next to the other request-body validations (email, postcode, telephone, missing fields), and it's wired as an escalation exactly like its peers rather than being handled inline. Logic is where a maintainer would look for it.\n- **No catch-all drift.** `ValidateOwner` is already the single validation step for the owner body; adding one more guarded check (with a clear comment) is the intended growth path, not a new responsibility.\n- **Consistent with siblings.** `OwnerRegistrationDateFutureException` and its handler match `OwnerEmailInvalidException(Handler)` / `OwnerPostcodeInvalidException(Handler)` to the line \u2014 same constructor/getter shape, same `errors`-array 400 body, same javadoc style. A maintainer would find it immediately familiar.\n- **Idiomatic OfficeFloor.** Checked exception in the `throws` clause, handler registered via `officefloor/escalation/<FQCN>.yml`, response sent through `ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<...>>` \u2014 all per the project conventions.\n\nNo duplication introduced, nothing misplaced.",
